WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 282: Duke Ellington: The Cotton Club, Take the A Train, and the Master of American Orchestration

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:20.000
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series celebrates the peerless composer, band leader, and aristocrat of American music who composed the soundscapes of modern urban sophistication.

4
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.560
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of Duke Ellington.

5
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:38.960
[George] Born in April 1899 into a cultured, middle class black family in Washington, D.C., Edward Kennedy Ellington was raised with the refined manners and aristocratic dignity of a prince.

6
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.320
[George] His father, James Edward, was a skilled blueprint maker and occasional white house butler.

7
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:50.240
[George] His mother Daisy surrounded young Edward with unconditional love and classical piano instruction.

8
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:58.000
[George] His dapper dress, flawless etiquette, and magnetic charisma earned him the childhood nickname that stayed with him for life.

9
00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:58.960
[George] Duke.

10
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:08.400
[George] Dropping out of high school just months before graduation to pursue music, Ellington learned the dazzling stride piano style from master pianists like James P.

11
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:14.800
[George] Johnson, painting commercial signs by day and playing ragtime at private society parties by night.

12
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:25.200
[George] In 1923, Duke packed his bags and moved to New York City with his band, the Washingtonians, diving headlong into the artistic renaissance of Harlem.

13
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:39.840
[George] Growing up in the thriving black middle class neighborhood of Shaw in Washington, D.C., young Edward was shielded from the harshest indignities of Southern segregation by parents who instilled in him an aristocratic sense of racial pride and self worth.

14
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:49.360
[George] His mother Daisy kept him in immaculate velvet suits and silk ties, reminding him daily that he was blessed by God and had a noble destiny to fulfill.

15
00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.240
[George] Studying the intricate stride piano patterns of Harvey Brooks and Doc Perry.

16
00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:06.920
[George] Ellington learned to roll bass lines with his left hand while decorating melodies with sparkling right hand flourishes, winning his first public acclaim playing at garden parties and society balls for Washington's elite.

17
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:19.240
[George] By age 20, Duke was operating his own successful music booking service, dispatching multiple ensembles across the District of Columbia and earning enough capital to buy a house for his parents.

18
00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:28.840
[George] In December 1927, Ellington's orchestra secured the coveted house band residency at Harlem's premier nightlife venue, the Cotton Club.

19
00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:39.640
[George] While the venue was strictly segregated, catering exclusively to wealthy white patrons while featuring black entertainers, Ellington turned the residency into an artistic laboratory.

20
00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:55.880
[George] Developing what critics termed the jungle sound, Ellington orchestrated daring, exotic tonal colors, Bubber Miley's growling, plunger-muted trumpet, tricky Sam Nanton's vocalized trombone wails, and Sonny Greer's shimmering percussive chimes.

21
00:02:55.880 --> 00:03:12.520
[George] Broadcast live across the nation over CBS Radio Network wires twice a week, Ellington's music mesmerized millions of late-night listeners, transforming Duke into a household name and shattering racial stereotypes with his peerless elegance and compositional brilliance.

22
00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:27.000
[George] The Cotton Club was a gilded pressure cooker of high society glamour and organized crime control, operated by underworld kingpin Oni Madden, who demanded exotic, theatrical musical numbers to accompany provocative floor reviews.

23
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:41.720
[George] Ellington transformed these sensationalist commercial demands into profound compositional triumphs, utilizing muted brass instruments, unusual harmonic scales, and African inspired percussion to invent the legendary jungle sound.

24
00:03:41.720 --> 00:04:00.720
[George] His radio broadcasts from the club reached across racial and geographic divides, captivating listeners in Midwestern farmhouses and urban penthouses alike, proving that black orchestral musicians were capable of peerless technical precision, harmonic elegance, and sophisticated artistic expression.

25
00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:11.120
[George] The national broadcasts from Lennox Avenue made Ellington an international icon of American modernism, celebrated by European classical critics as a master harmonist.

26
00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:21.280
[George] Unlike classical composers who wrote abstract scores for anonymous musicians, Ellington's true instrument was the individual human personalities of his orchestra members.

27
00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:43.040
[George] He composed specifically for the distinctive sonic colors of each player, the silky, swooping alto saxophone glissandos of Johnny Hodges, the deep, resonant anchor of Harry Carney's baritone saxophone, who rode in Duke's car and remained in the band for 47 years, and the fiery, passionate trumpet blasts of Cootie Williams and Rex Stewart.

28
00:04:43.040 --> 00:05:04.320
[George] Composing at night in rolling Pullman railroad cars to avoid segregated Southern hotels, Ellington penned an unbroken stream of American standards, mood indigo, sophisticated lady, it don't mean a thing if it ain't got that swing, and in a sentimental mood, establishing jazz as an art form of exquisite melodic refinement.

29
00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:15.040
[George] Ellington's orchestral method was completely unique in the history of music, where European classical composers wrote abstract lines that could be played by any skilled symphony musician.

30
00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:21.760
[George] Ellington composed around the specific timbers, eccentricities, and tonal flaws of his individual players.

31
00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:44.680
[George] He wrote soaring romantic ballads to showcase Johnny Hodges' weeping alto saxophone glissandos, constructed intricate low-register counterpoints around Harry Carney's velvety baritone horn, and layered dissonant trumpet mutes over Sonny Greer's shimmering cymbals to create atmospheric moods of melancholy and mystery that came to be celebrated worldwide as the Ellington Effect.

32
00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:54.360
[George] Duke kept his star soloists fiercely loyal through decades of touring, paying them top salaries out of his own royalties even during lean economic depressions.

33
00:05:54.360 --> 00:06:03.080
[George] In December 1938, Ellington met a diminutive, 23-year-old Pittsburgh pianist and composer named Billy Strayhorn.

34
00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:07.400
[George] It was one of the most fruitful creative partnerships in musical history.

35
00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:15.880
[George] Strayhorn became Duke's alter ego, arranger, and co-composer, seamlessly finishing each other's musical thoughts without ego.

36
00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:26.280
[George] Armed with Ellington's handwritten travel directions to Harlem, Take Through a Train Strahorn composed the iconic, swinging theme that became the orchestra's signature anthem.

37
00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:32.280
[George] Together, Ellington and Strayhorn pushed big band jazz beyond the three-minute pop song format.

38
00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:46.920
[George] Composing monumental extended masterpieces like Black, Brown and Beige, a monumental musical tone parallel to the history of the American Negro, which premiered before a sold-out audience at Carnegie Hall in January 1943.

39
00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:58.120
[George] The meeting between Ellington and Billy Strayhorn in 1938 was a fateful convergence of two musical souls who shared an identical harmonic vocabulary and aesthetic vision.

40
00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:12.120
[George] Strayhorn was a classically trained prodigy whose sophisticated knowledge of European impressionist harmony, particularly the works of Ravel and Debussy, perfectly complemented Ellington's intuitive blues roots and orchestral mastery.

41
00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:28.880
[George] Their collaborative suites, including the nutcracker Suite and Such Sweet Thunder based on Shakespeare's plays, dismantled the boundaries separating classical art music from jazz, proving that big band orchestration was an American fine art equal to any European symphony.

42
00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:40.080
[George] In Lush Life and Chelsea Bridge, Strayhorn contributed delicate, melancholy masterpieces that enriched the orchestra's repertoire with unprecedented emotional and harmonic nuance.

43
00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:53.360
[George] By the mid-1950s, the rise of rock and roll, television, and small bebop combos had caused big bands across America to dissolve in bankruptcy, leaving Ellington's orchestra struggling for bookings.

44
00:07:53.360 --> 00:08:04.240
[George] But on the cool midnight of July 7, 1956, at the Newport Jazz Festival in Rhode Island, Duke engineered the most legendary comeback in jazz history.

45
00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:19.440
[George] Launching into his 1937 blues number, Diminuendo and Crescendo in Blue, tenor saxophonist Paul Gonsalves stepped to the microphone and unleashed a searing, 27-chorus improvised solo of raw, volcanic swing.

46
00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:26.080
[George] Seven thousand spectators leapt onto their chairs, dancing in the aisles in a frenzy of ecstatic excitement.

47
00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:34.880
[George] The performance landed Duke on the cover of Time magazine, revitalizing his career and opening a glorious two-decade autumn of global acclaim.

48
00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:50.080
[George] The legendary 1956 Newport Jazz Festival performance occurred at a critical crossroads when critics were dismissing Ellington as an aging relic of the pre-war big band era, with several band members contemplating quitting due to dwindling tour revenues.

49
00:08:50.080 --> 00:09:03.320
[George] When tenor saxophonist Paul Gonsalves began his epic 27-chorus blues solo between the two halves of Diminuendo and Crescendo in Blue, the electrical charge in the outdoor arena became uncontrollable.

50
00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:18.440
[George] A platinum blonde socialite in a black evening gown leapt onto a wooden box to dance, inciting the entire crowd of 7,000 people to surge toward the stage in an ecstasy of swinging joy, instantly reviving Duke's international career.

51
00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:29.000
[George] Festival organizer George Wine later recalled that the performance was the most electrifying moment in the history of jazz, proving that Ellington's music was timeless and invincible.

52
00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:42.920
[George] In his final decades, Ellington traveled the world, drawing inspiration from international tours to compose ambitious extended suites, the Far East Suite, the Latin American Suite, and the New Orleans Suite.

53
00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:51.240
[George] When critics attempted to categorize his music as jazz, Duke gently rebuffed them, declaring his art to be beyond category.

54
00:09:51.240 --> 00:10:07.080
[George] Turning his profound personal faith into music, Ellington composed three monumental sacred concerts between 1965 and 1973, performing them in majestic cathedrals from San Francisco's Grace Cathedral to London's Westminster Abbey.

55
00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:18.680
[George] Featuring choir, orchestra and tap dancing, Duke called these sacred compositions the most important thing I have ever done, offering his immense musical gifts directly to his creator.

56
00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:31.640
[George] In his later years, Ellington's compositions became increasingly sweeping and cosmopolitan, drawing harmonic inspiration from his State Department tour through Syria, India, Japan, and Latin America.

57
00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:47.280
[George] In his three monumental sacred concerts, Ellington fused biblical psalms, gospel chorals, jazz orchestration, and tap dancing into grand spiritual offerings, declaring that no artist could ever repay the divine creator for the gift of music.

58
00:10:47.520 --> 00:11:00.400
[George] Performing these massive works in ancient European cathedrals and American churches, Ellington demonstrated that jazz could be a sacred vehicle of divine worship, elevating human souls toward heavenly grace.

59
00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:09.120
[George] Duke's sacred music was an expression of profound personal devotion, bringing audiences of all religious faiths together in shared reverence.

60
00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:23.760
[George] In April 1969, for his 70th birthday, President Richard Nixon hosted a historic White House Gala in Ellington's honor, awarding him the Presidential Medal of Freedom, the highest civilian award of the United States.

61
00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:35.440
[George] Nixon sat at the piano to play Happy Birthday, and Duke greeted the president with his trademark Four Kisses, two on each cheek, symbolizing love, art, courage, and beauty.

62
00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:45.680
[George] Duke Ellington passed away in May 1974 at age 75, leaving behind over 2,000 recorded works that anchor the foundation of American culture.

63
00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:48.800
[George] Over 12,000 mourners crowded the Cathedral of St.

64
00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:50.960
[George] John the Divine for his funeral.

65
00:11:50.960 --> 00:12:02.240
[George] Duke Ellington proved that America possessed a classical music of its own, forged in the fires of black genius, urban sophistication, and the eternal, unfettered freedom of swing.

66
00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:20.960
[George] At the glittering White House Gala in April 1969, celebrating Duke's 70th birthday, President Nixon awarded him the nation's highest civilian honor, while legendary musicians including Dave Brubeck, Dizzy Gillespie, and Earl Hines performed Ellington's timeless standards in the East Room.

67
00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:27.200
[George] When Duke passed away in May 1974, over twelve thousand mourners filled the Cathedral of St.

68
00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:33.160
[George] John the Divine to bid farewell to the aristocratic maestro whose music had defined an entire century.

69
00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:46.520
[George] Duke Ellington composed the definitive soundtrack of American urban sophistication, leaving behind a monumental legacy of over 2,000 compositions that remain an eternal monument to the triumph of the human imagination.

70
00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:55.880
[George] His music stands alongside the greatest orchestral achievements in world history, a testament to American creativity and cultural majesty.

71
00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:08.840
[Rebecca] Duke Ellington's peerless sophistication, prolific genius, and aristocratic grace elevated American jazz into a timeless classical art form.

72
00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:18.440
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with the grand finale of our top 50 American series, honoring the legendary visionaries who forged the modern world.

73
00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:28.360
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

